secretweb ({{ secretweb_env }}) - quick reference
==================================================

This install:   {{ secretweb_install_dir }}
Service user:   {{ secretweb_user }}
systemd unit:   {{ secretweb_unit_name }}
mTLS port:      {{ secretweb_port }}

First-time setup (once per host)
---------------------------------

Run this on every host in the network - the SAME full host list gets
entered on every one, in separate/mirrored terminal sessions. Don't try
to special-case "this host" - it's detected automatically from this
host's own certificate.

    sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/venv/bin/python3 {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/setup_secretweb.py

({{ secretweb_user }}'s shell is /usr/sbin/nologin, so `su - {{ secretweb_user }}`
or `sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} -i` won't work - the command above runs the
script directly instead, unaffected by that.)

It will, in order: collect the host list; generate KEY1 and write the
encrypted data files; start a temporary server; wait for every other host
to come online; Shamir-split KEY1 and publish one share to each other
host; then ask you to confirm every OTHER host is either still safely at
this same point or already fully switched to systemd, stop the temporary
server, and tell you to run:

    sudo systemctl start {{ secretweb_unit_name }}
    sudo systemctl status {{ secretweb_unit_name }}

Do this ONE HOST AT A TIME - move to the next host only once this one's
systemd service is confirmed running.

Creating a secret
-------------------

certificates/private.pem is only readable by {{ secretweb_user }}
(mode 600), so run the client as that user either way. It figures out
which hosts to publish to, and how many shares/what treshold, from
hosts.json on its own - no need to name peers or point at cert files
by hand.

{% if secretweb_symlink_client %}
    echo -n 'the secret value' | sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} secretweb-client store-secret --name my-secret
{% else %}
No /usr/local/bin symlink in this environment - call secretweb_client.py directly:

    echo -n 'the secret value' | sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/venv/bin/python3 {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/secretweb_client.py store-secret --name my-secret
{% endif %}

Piping the value in via stdin (as above) is deliberate - a `--secret`
option also exists but is visible to other local users via ps/proc while
the command runs, so stdin is the safer default. On success this prints
the uuid and how many/which hosts accepted a share; if fewer than the
required treshold accept it, the secret is reported as failed and is
*not* recorded as one of this host's own secrets.

Decrypting an existing secret isn't built yet - `store-secret` only
covers creation.

Lower-level: store/retrieve one already-made share against one named peer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is what `store-secret` above uses internally - reach for it directly
only for manual ops/debugging against a single host:

{% if secretweb_symlink_client %}
    sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} secretweb-client store-share --host <peer-address> --port {{ secretweb_port }} \
        --data-file share.json \
        --cert-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/cert.pem \
        --key-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/private.pem \
        --ca-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/ca.crt
{% else %}
    sudo -u {{ secretweb_user }} {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/venv/bin/python3 {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/peer_client.py store-share \
        --host <peer-address> --port {{ secretweb_port }} \
        --data-file share.json \
        --cert-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/cert.pem \
        --key-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/private.pem \
        --ca-file {{ secretweb_install_dir }}/certificates/ca.crt
{% endif %}

(`get-share` works the same way, with `--uuid <share-uuid>` instead of
`--data-file`.)

Stopping / restarting
-----------------------

    sudo systemctl stop {{ secretweb_unit_name }}
    sudo systemctl restart {{ secretweb_unit_name }}

(this calls `initiator.py --stop` under the hood - see ExecStop in the
unit file at /etc/systemd/system/{{ secretweb_unit_name }}.service.)
